<v Samantha>Yes.
<v Samantha>是的。

00:41:30.770 --> 00:41:31.720
<v Craig Findley>90 days from today, the--
<v Craig Findley>自今天起 90 天后——

00:41:31.720 --> 00:42:11.390
<v Ben Austen>After 48 years of incarceration, Henry Dee was going free. The whole thing took less than an hour. Of the seven cases the board considers today, this is the only one granted release, and I can't stop thinking about it. It's all my producer, Bill, and I talk about on the three-hour drive back from Springfield to Chicago, because we just saw a long prison sentence end. It's impossible to sit through these hearings and not think about what decision you would make.
<v Ben Austen>在 48 年监禁后，亨利·迪将获得自由。整个过程不到一小时。在委员会今天审议的七个案件中，这是唯一一个获释的案例，我无法停止思考。在我从斯普林菲尔德回芝加哥的三个小时车程里，我和我的制片人比尔都在谈论这件事，因为我们刚目睹了一个长期的刑期结束。参加这些听证会而不去想自己会做什么决定是不可能的。

00:42:11.390 --> 00:42:42.380
<v Ben Austen>For me, there's no question-- I would have voted for release. And I want to be clear about what it means to say that, because two people were murdered, and I don't think we'll ever know what happened. I'm saying that even if Henry Dee did kill Edith and Arthur Snyder back in 1971 and lied about it all these years, even then, to me, it was time to let Henry Dee out. Past time.
<v Ben Austen>对我来说，毫无疑问——我会投赞成释放票。我想明确说明这意味着什么，因为有两人被谋杀，我认为我们永远不会知道发生了什么。我的意思是，即使亨利·迪在 1971 年确实杀了伊迪丝和亚瑟·斯奈德并谎报了这么多年，即便那样，对我来说，也是时候放亨利·迪出去了。早该放了。

00:42:42.380 --> 00:43:13.300
<v Ben Austen>We spent a long time trying to get in touch with the family of Edith and Arthur Snyder. I did eventually reach their daughter. She's now about 80 years old. She told me she strongly opposed Dee's release, had written a letter to the board before this hearing and for nearly every one of his hearings over the years. She believes he's guilty. And she says, even if you're a saint in prison, if you take a life, it's forever. So you should never be let out.
<v Ben Austen>我们花了很长时间试图联系伊迪丝和亚瑟·斯奈德的家人。我最终联系上了他们的女儿。她现在大约 80 岁。她告诉我她强烈反对迪的释放，在这次听证会前以及过去多年的每次听证会上都写过信给委员会。她认为他有罪。她说，即使你在狱中是圣人，如果你夺走了一条生命，那是永远的。所以永远不应该被放出来。

00:43:13.300 --> 00:43:35.680
<v Ben Austen>We talked for a while about parole, about our different views on punishment. She said, had your loved ones been killed, maybe you'd feel the same way I do. I told her that was a fair point. I still believe what I believe, but it's true. I haven't gone through what she has.
<v Ben Austen>我们谈论了一会儿假释，谈论了我们对惩罚的不同看法。她说，如果你的亲人被杀，也许你的感受会和我一样。我告诉她这是一个公平的观点。我仍然相信我的信念，但那是事实。我没有经历过她所经历的一切。

00:43:35.680 --> 00:44:15.060
<v Ben Austen>I didn't say this earlier, but Illinois is one of the states that got rid of parole, way back in 1978. So only people like Henry Dee, sentenced before 1978, are still eligible for hearings like this one. So the people who come before this board are mostly in their 60s and above. They're senior citizens, which means, on any actuarial table, they pose very little risk of committing another crime. And still, the parole board only says yes to a tiny percentage-- in the 15 years before Dee's hearing, just 6% of them.
<v Ben Austen>我早先没提过，但伊利诺伊州是早在 1978 年就废除假释制度的州之一。所以只有像亨利·迪这样在 1978 年前被判刑的人才有资格参加这样的听证会。所以出现在这个委员会面前的人大多在 60 岁以上。他们是老年公民，这意味着，在任何精算表中，他们再犯罪的风险都很小。尽管如此，假释委员会批准的百分比仍然很少——在迪参加听证会前的 15 年里，只有 6%。

00:44:15.060 --> 00:44:41.840
<v Ben Austen>So who makes the cut? In the numerous cases I've seen, the people who did get released, there's always a story that enough board members were able to latch onto, something that allowed them, finally, to move beyond that magnetic pull of the long-ago crime. In Henry Dee's case, it was the unbridgeable distance between the brutality of the murders and the peacefulness of his life in prison.
<v Ben Austen>那么谁能通过呢？在我看过的众多案件中，那些获释的人，总有一个故事让足够多的委员能够抓住，某种东西最终让他们超越了对多年前罪行的磁吸力。在亨利·迪的案例中，那是谋杀的残暴与他狱中生活的平静之间无法逾越的距离。

00:44:41.840 --> 00:44:54.410
<v Ben Austen>Another guy I saw get paroled, he had a surgery that left him bleeding in his cell for years. The horror of his medical care in prison made his offense seem to the board almost beside the point.
<v Ben Austen>我见过的另一个获得假释的人，他曾做过手术，多年来在牢房里一直流血。他在狱中医疗护理的可怕状况使他的罪行在委员会看来几乎无关紧要。

00:44:54.410 --> 00:45:17.520
<v Ben Austen>And then this other time, a man not only escaped, but lived under an alias in a different state for years. He was a beloved member of that community before he was apprehended again. The board, to my shock, saw that time when he was at large like a test case for the positive life he'd live if they gave him another chance.
<v Ben Austen>还有一次，一个人不仅逃跑了，还在另一个州以化名生活了多年。在被捕之前，他一直是那个社区深受爱戴的成员。委员会，令我震惊的是，把他在逃的那段时间看作是如果给他第二次机会，他会过上积极生活的一个测试案例。

00:45:17.520 --> 00:45:33.010
<v Ben Austen>I've thought a lot about whether Illinois and the rest of the country should bring back parole, whether it makes sense to get more people in front of boards again. Parole decisions can be racially biased, completely focused on the original crime, and just random.
<v Ben Austen>我经常思考伊利诺伊州和全国是否应该恢复假释制度，让更多人再次出现在委员会面前是否合理。假释决定可能带有种族偏见，完全集中在最初的罪行上，而且非常随机。

00:45:33.010 --> 00:46:06.310
<v Ben Austen>But even knowing all this, I think we need more systems of second chances. The United States locks up more people than any other country, about one in six of all the incarcerated people in the world. There are hundreds of thousands of people in prisons like Henry Dee. They've spent decades behind bars. Many will die there. They deserve another look.
<v Ben Austen>但即使了解这一切，我认为我们需要更多的第二次机会系统。美国监禁的人数超过任何其他国家，约占世界所有在押人员的六分之一。监狱里有数十万像亨利·迪这样的人。他们已经在铁窗后度过了几十年。许多人将死在那里。他们值得重新审视。

00:46:06.310 --> 00:46:16.070
<v Ira Glass>When we come back, Ben tries to track down Henry Dee. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>回来后，本将尝试寻找亨利·迪。稍后，在节目继续时，来自芝加哥公共广播电台。

00:46:16.070 --> 00:46:21.390
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Ben Austen picks up the story again.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。本·奥斯汀再次接续这个故事。

00:46:21.390 --> 00:46:33.210
<v Ben Austen>Even after sitting through Henry Dee's parole hearing, I didn't really know him. I hadn't even seen or heard him. So I tried to find out more. Here's what I learned.
<v Ben Austen>即使在旁听了亨利·迪的假释听证会之后，我还是不真正了解他。我甚至没见过也没听过他。所以我试图了解更多。这是我学到的。

00:46:33.210 --> 00:46:54.270
<v Ben Austen>After Henry Dee makes parole, people at the prison celebrate, even the staff. And the day he walks out the front gate, he's getting hugs and congratulations from everyone. His mother's still alive, in her 90s. She's not walking. She can't meet him there. But Henry is ecstatic to see her.
<v Ben Austen>亨利·迪获得假释后，监狱里的人们甚至工作人员都为他庆祝。在他走出前门的那天，每个人都在拥抱他并表示祝贺。他的母亲还活着，90 多岁了。她不能走路。她无法在那里迎接他。但亨利看到她非常狂喜。

00:46:54.270 --> 00:47:25.950
<v Ben Austen>He gets outside-- his first free air in 48 years-- and there are federal officers waiting for him. They handcuff him, put him in a car. Henry has no idea what's going on. He eventually learns it's for one of the attempted escapes all those decades ago. He's been granted parole by the state, but the feds are tacking on two more years. He's driven to a federal prison in Pennsylvania. His mom dies during that time.
<v Ben Austen>他走出室外——这是他 48 年来第一次呼吸自由的空气——联邦官员在那里等他。他们给他戴上手铐，把他放进车里。亨利不知道发生了什么。他最终得知这是因为几十年前的某次逃跑未遂。他获得了州的假释，但联邦政府加了两年刑期。他被驱逐到宾夕法尼亚州的一所联邦监狱。在那期间，他妈妈去世了。

00:47:25.950 --> 00:47:45.510
<v Ben Austen>27 months later, the feds put him on a plane to Chicago. He's never flown before, has no idea how to board, where to sit. The flight attendants learn his story and move him to first class. He has to turn down the free drinks because he doesn't want to violate his parole.
<v Ben Austen>27 个月后，联邦政府把他送上了飞往芝加哥的飞机。他从没坐过飞机，不知道怎么登机，坐在哪里。空乘人员得知了他的故事，把他移到了头等舱。他不得不拒绝免费饮料，因为他不想违反假释规定。

00:47:45.510 --> 00:48:08.040
<v Ben Austen>From O'Hare, he gets on a train. He's got directions written out on paper. He doesn't have a cell phone. He makes it to a Salvation Army on the city's West Side. That's where he sleeps. He later moves to a homeless shelter. The friend from inside who won the wrongful conviction suit who promised Henry a job and a home, it doesn't pan out.
<v Ben Austen>从奥黑尔机场出来，他上了火车。他手里拿着写在纸上的路线说明。他没有手机。他到了城市西区的一家救世军收容所。那是他睡觉的地方。后来他搬到了一个无家可归者收容所。那位赢了诉讼并承诺给亨利工作和住所的狱中老友，最终没能兑现。

00:48:08.040 --> 00:48:41.710
<v Ben Austen>So Henry's trying to figure out his new surroundings, his new life, but he has medical issues. Eventually, he's admitted to the hospital. He has diabetes, fluid on his lungs. And after a few days there, he's dead. Henry Dee was incarcerated for 50 years. He lives free after his release for less than 12 months.
<v Ben Austen>所以亨利试图弄清楚他的新环境、他的新生活，但他有医疗问题。最终，他被送进了医院。他有糖尿病，肺部积水。几天后，他死了。亨利·迪被监禁了 50 年。他在获释后自由地生活了不到 12 个月。

00:48:41.710 --> 00:49:07.840
<v Ben Austen>I tracked down a few people who knew Henry Dee well. I want to take these last few minutes to tell you some things about him. Henry Dee wore a frog pin around the prison. He'd leave the cell house, walk onto the yard, and dozens of stray cats would appear. He'd feed them. He was a large man, hands like catcher's mitts. When young guys asked him who his gang chief was, he'd tell them his mom.
<v Ben Austen>我追踪了几个了解亨利·迪的人。我想利用最后几分钟告诉你们关于他的一些事。亨利·迪在监狱周围戴着一枚青蛙胸针。他会离开牢房，走进院子，几十只流浪猫就会出现。他会喂它们。他是个大块头，手像接球手的手套一样大。当年轻人问他谁是帮派老大时，他会告诉他们是他妈妈。

00:49:07.840 --> 00:49:23.010
<v Ben Austen>Because of his diabetes, he could get jittery or pass out on the toilet in his cell, and his friends were always on watch for him. For nine years, the person who lived with him in a six by eight foot cell was a guy named Jacob Rivera.
<v Ben Austen>由于糖尿病，他会在牢房的马桶上变得焦躁或昏倒，他的朋友们总是为他守夜。九年来，那个与他在 6 英尺乘 8 英尺牢房里一起生活的人叫雅各布·里维拉。

00:49:23.010 --> 00:49:42.000
<v Jacob Rivera>Henry was funny, man. I would sit in a cell on lockdowns and write these letters. And not having a very good educational background, whoever I was writing to, I tried to make it seem like I was educated. And I would try to use these big words.
<v Jacob Rivera>亨利很有趣，伙计。我在封锁期间会坐在牢房里写信。而且没有很好的教育背景，无论我写给谁，我都试图让我看起来很有学问。我会试着用那些大词。

00:49:42.000 --> 00:50:02.240
<v Jacob Rivera>And I told Henry-- hey, Henry, what word can I use for blah, blah, blah, blah, blah? He's like, what are you trying to say? And then I would tell him what I was trying to say, and he'll say, well, just say that. [LAUGHS] You know? And I'm like, oh, yeah. Yeah, he had a way.
<v Jacob Rivera>我告诉亨利——嘿，亨利，我可以用什么词来代替“巴拉巴拉巴拉”？他就像，你想说什么？然后我告诉他我想表达什么，他说，好吧，直接说出来就好。[笑] 你知道？我就想，哦，是的。是的，他有他的一套方法。

00:50:02.240 --> 00:50:21.120
<v Ben Austen>Jacob called him Grandpa. A chaplain at the prison told me she called him Father Abraham because she saw him as a man of wisdom and love. Those times he came up for parole, the entire prison felt hope. Here's another man who was locked up with him, Andre Ruddock.
<v Ben Austen>雅各布叫他爷爷。监狱的一位牧师告诉我，她叫他亚伯拉罕父亲，因为她视他为一个充满智慧和爱的人。他参加假释听证会的时候，整个监狱都感到希望。这是另一个和他一起被关押的人，安德烈·拉多克。

00:50:21.120 --> 00:50:37.030
<v Andre Ruddock>All of the guards, all of the officers, all the way up to the warden, everybody would get engaged. Like, everybody knew, Henry Dee goes to the board. He's going to the board. They might let him out, finally. This might be his year.
<v Andre Ruddock>所有的看守，所有的警官，一直到典狱长，每个人都会参与进来。每个人都知道，亨利·迪去听证会了。他要去听证会了。他们可能最终会放他出去。这可能是他的一年。

00:50:37.030 --> 00:50:59.170
<v Ben Austen>When Henry Dee finally got released, Andre was already out. And when he learned that Henry was sleeping in a homeless shelter, he was furious. So Andre raced over to the shelter, called some people, and they got Henry Dee an apartment. They recorded a video of him seeing it for the first time. Here he is, Henry Dee.
<v Ben Austen>当亨利·迪最终获释时，安德烈已经出狱了。当他得知亨利睡在无家可归者收容所时，他非常愤怒。所以安德烈赶到收容所，给一些人打电话，他们给亨利·迪弄了一间公寓。他们录了一段他第一次看到它的视频。这是他，亨利·迪。

00:50:59.170 --> 00:51:00.830
<v Woman 1>That's your living room, all right?
<v Woman 1>那是你的客厅，对吗？

00:51:00.830 --> 00:51:02.150
<v Henry Dee>Yeah, that's my living room.
<v Henry Dee>是的，那是我的客厅。

00:51:02.150 --> 00:51:03.020
<v Woman 1>This is your little kitchen area.
<v Woman 1>这是你的小厨房区域。

00:51:03.020 --> 00:51:04.720
<v Henry Dee>Ooh, Lord.
<v Henry Dee>哦，主啊。

00:51:04.720 --> 00:51:06.520
<v Woman 1>You got a brand-new bathroom.
<v Woman 1>你有一个全新的浴室。

00:51:06.520 --> 00:51:08.480
<v Henry Dee>A brand-new bathroom! Wow.
<v Henry Dee>全新的浴室！哇。

00:51:08.480 --> 00:51:09.190
<v Woman 2>This is nice.
<v Woman 2>这很好。

00:51:09.190 --> 00:51:09.770
<v Andre Ruddock>Look at it.
<v Andre Ruddock>看看它。

00:51:09.770 --> 00:51:15.850
<v Henry Dee>Well, this is wonderful. This is more room than I done had in the last century.
<v Henry Dee>嗯，这太美妙了。这比我过去一个世纪拥有的空间还要多。

00:51:15.850 --> 00:51:31.960
<v Ben Austen>Henry has a full white beard. He's wearing a wool hat and a gray hoodie. And he uses a walker as he enters the remodeled kitchen. There are granite countertops. Everything is bright and freshly painted. Henry is jubilant. They all are.
<v Ben Austen>亨利长着浓密的白胡子。他戴着羊毛帽，穿着灰色连帽衫。当他走进改造过的厨房时，他拄着助行器。有花岗岩台面。一切都很明亮且刚刚粉刷过。亨利欣喜若狂。他们都是。

00:51:31.960 --> 00:51:33.700
<v Andre Ruddock>Come look at your bathroom.
<v Andre Ruddock>来看看你的浴室。

00:51:33.700 --> 00:51:35.810
<v Henry Dee>Man, I see it all from right here.
<v Henry Dee>伙计，我从这儿就能看见全部。

00:51:35.810 --> 00:51:36.460
<v Henry Dee>[LAUGHTER]
<v Henry Dee>[笑声]

00:51:36.460 --> 00:51:42.730
<v Henry Dee>I've been living in a bathroom smaller than this all my life. Whew, look at that.
<v Henry Dee>我一辈子都住在比这小的浴室里。噢，看看那个。

00:51:42.730 --> 00:51:44.150
<v Woman 1>Hopefully, you'll enjoy your space.
<v Woman 1>希望你能享受你的空间。

00:51:44.150 --> 00:51:45.950
<v Henry Dee>Oh, I do. I enjoy it already.
<v Henry Dee>哦，我享受。我已经很享受了。

00:51:45.950 --> 00:51:46.600
<v Andre Ruddock>Nice.
<v Andre Ruddock>很好。

00:51:46.600 --> 00:51:57.680
<v Henry Dee>You've done a wonderful job. Thank you so much. What's your name?
<v Henry Dee>你做得太棒了。非常感谢。你叫什么名字？

00:51:57.680 --> 00:52:17.630
<v Ben Austen>Henry had 351 days of freedom. I saw a video of him from one of those days. He's surrounded by three little dogs leaping all over him, and he's giddy. He loved to play the lottery. And he had this new makeshift family-- the people who knew him in prison who now just wanted to be around him.
<v Ben Austen>亨利有 351 天的自由。我看过他其中一天的视频。他被三只围着他跳来跳去的小狗包围着，他非常兴奋。他喜欢买彩票。他有了这个临时的家庭——那些在狱中了解他的人，现在只想和他在一起。

00:52:17.630 --> 00:52:34.520
<v Ben Austen>But he never slept in the new apartment. At night, he'd go back to the homeless shelter. For five decades, he'd been surrounded by hundreds of people. He was terrified of being alone. Andre told me that in prison, Henry was a giant.
<v Ben Austen>但他从没在那个新公寓里睡觉。晚上，他会回到无家可归者收容所。在过去的五十年里，他一直被几百人包围着。他害怕独自一人。安德烈告诉我，在狱中，亨利是个巨人。

00:52:34.520 --> 00:52:49.530
<v Andre Ruddock>After all that, after all that he survived, he was a powerhouse. This is what we all knew. Henry Dee was so big. And he talk about his hands big. He had this deep, powerful voice. This was the character.
<v Andre Ruddock>在经历了这一切，在所有这些他生存下来之后，他是个能量中心。这是我们都知道的。亨利·迪太伟大了。他谈论他的大手。他有一种深沉、有力的声音。这就是这个角色。

00:52:49.530 --> 00:53:09.730
<v Andre Ruddock>But then he came home, and the real world shrunk him. It shrunk him and then defeated him. And he never got a chance to do anything that he really wanted to do. And one of the things he told me when he was in the hospital, that he wanted to go to the observation deck on Willis Tower.
<v Andre Ruddock>但他回家了，真实世界缩减了他。它缩减了他，然后击败了他。他从来没机会做任何他真正想做的事。在他住院时他告诉我的一件事，就是他想去威利斯大厦的观景台。

00:53:09.730 --> 00:53:15.070
<v Ben Austen>The Willis Tower, also known as the Sears Tower, is Chicago's tallest building.
<v Ben Austen>威利斯大厦，也就是西尔斯大厦，是芝加哥最高的建筑。

00:53:15.070 --> 00:53:45.460
<v Andre Ruddock>I told him that's the first thing we're going to do when you get out of the hospital. I'm going to take you up there. But since he died, he obviously couldn't. So I took his picture that you have, that obituary. I took it up to the observation deck and took a picture of it. [CRYING] Like, yeah, Henry Dee, you made it. We got you up here, so. [CLEARS THROAT]
<v Andre Ruddock>我告诉他那是你出院后我们做的第一件事。我要带你上去。但因为他死了，他显然不能去了。所以我拿着你有的那张照片，那个讣告。我把它带到观景台拍了张照片。[哭泣] 是的，亨利·迪，你成功了。我们把你带到这儿了，所以。[清嗓子]

00:53:45.460 --> 00:54:11.080
<v Ben Austen>As far as I can tell, Henry Dee out of prison was the same person Virginia Martinez saw in prison, the same person prison officials had been describing to the parole board for decades.
<v Ben Austen>据我所知，出狱后的亨利·迪和马丁内斯在监狱里看到的是同一个人，是监狱官员几十年来一直向假释委员会描述的同一个人。

00:54:11.080 --> 00:54:18.100
<v Ira Glass>Ben Austen. He wrote a book about the parole system and the odysseys of two men trying to go free. It's called Correction.
<v Ira Glass>本·奥斯汀。他写了一本关于假释制度和两个试图获得自由的人的奥德赛的书。书名叫《纠正》。

00:54:18.100 --> 00:54:41.820
<v Ira Glass>He also hosts a new podcast called The Parole Room, which centers around a different case than the one you just heard-- a kind of famous case in Chicago, actually-- with lots of twists and turns, including a guy on parole who gets out and then insists on attending parole hearings with Ben. You can find all eight episodes right now at audible.com/parole. This story was produced and edited by our senior editor, David Kestenbaum.
<v Ira Glass>他还主持了一个名为《假释室》的新播客，内容集中在一个与你刚才听到的完全不同的案件上——实际上是芝加哥一个很有名的案子——有很多转折和起伏，包括一个获释后坚持和本一起参加假释听证会的假释者。你现在可以在 audible.com/parole 找到所有八集。这个故事是由我们的高级编辑大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆制作和编辑的。

00:54:41.820 --> 00:55:16.180
<v Ira Glass>["PAROLE SONG" BY SLAUGHTER BEACH, DOG]
<v Ira Glass>["假释之歌" - SLAUGHTER BEACH, DOG]

00:55:16.180 --> 00:55:30.500
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Aviva DeKornfeld. The people who put together today's show include Sean Cole, Michael Comite, Henry Larson, Seth Lind, Katherine Rae Mondo, Stowe Nelson, Nadia Reiman, Ryan Rumery, Alissa Shipp, and Matt Tierney. Our managing editor is Sarah Abdurrahman. Our executive editor is Emanuele Berry.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由阿维娃·德科恩菲尔德制作。今天节目组成员包括肖恩·科尔、迈克尔·科米特、亨利·拉尔森、塞思·林德、凯瑟琳·雷·蒙多、斯托·纳尔逊、纳迪亚·雷曼、瑞安·鲁梅里、阿丽莎·希普和马特·蒂尔尼。我们的总编辑是萨拉·阿卜杜拉赫曼。我们的执行编辑是伊曼纽尔·贝里。

00:55:30.500 --> 00:55:41.620
<v Ira Glass>Today's show was fact-checked by Christopher Swetala. He's the person on staff who first heard Ben's podcast and thought we might collaborate on a story with him. Fact-checking help from Heena Srivastava and Rudy Lee.
<v Ira Glass>今天节目的事实核查员是克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉。他是最早听到本的播客并想到我们可以和他合作一个故事的工作人员。事实核查帮助来自希娜·斯里瓦斯塔瓦和鲁迪·李。

00:55:41.620 --> 00:56:02.480
<v Ira Glass>In the years since his 2019 hearing that you just heard, one of the board members, Sal Diaz, has died. Special thanks today to Ben's producer, Bill Healy, to The Invisible Institute, who funded some of the early reporting that Ben and Bill did on this, to Sayre Quevedo, Lori Wilbert, Jason Sew Hoy, Josh Crist, Marciane Tanada, Lauren Olsen, Jelly Monteros, Ashley Lusk, and Jake Shapiro.
<v Ira Glass>在他 2019 年听证会后的几年里，委员之一萨尔·迪亚兹去世了。今天特别感谢本的制片人比尔·希利，感谢资助了本和比尔早期部分报道的“隐形研究所”，感谢塞尔·奎韦多、洛丽·威尔伯特、杰森·苏豪伊、乔什·克里斯特、马尔西亚娜·塔纳达、劳伦·奥尔森、杰莉·蒙特罗斯、阿什利·拉斯克和杰克·夏皮罗。

00:56:02.480 --> 00:56:19.170
<v Ira Glass>Thanks also to all of our Life Partners-- that is, everybody who has signed up for the new premium subscription version of our show, which you heard me mention at the top of the episode. You can join them-- join us-- as our partner in keeping the show strong at thisamericanlife.org/lifepartners.
<v Ira Glass>感谢我们所有的生活伙伴——也就是说，所有签署了我们节目的新高级订阅版本的人，我已经在节目开始时提到了。你可以加入他们——加入我们——成为我们保持节目强劲的伙伴，网址是 thisamericanlife.org/lifepartners。

00:56:19.170 --> 00:56:42.440
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's confounder, Mr. Torey Malatia. He actually screen tested, back in the day, for the little girl character in ET, the Drew Barrymore part. But the scene where they opened the closet and see ET and scream? Torey just could not deliver. They call action. He looks at ET and blurts out--
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由公共广播交换平台 PRX 传送到公共广播电台。像往常一样感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚先生。他年轻时确实试镜过《外星人 ET》里的小女孩角色，德鲁·巴里摩尔的部分。但在那个打开衣柜看到 ET 然后尖叫的场景中？托里就是演不出来。他们喊 action。他看着 ET 脱口而出——

00:56:42.440 --> 00:56:48.110
<v Sal Diaz>I liked the guy. He's impressed me. But he needs to go home.
<v Sal Diaz>我挺喜欢这个家伙的。他给我留下了深刻印象。但他需要回家。

00:56:48.110 --> 00:56:52.070
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带回《美国生活》的更多故事。

